WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.659
A quote service runs on a fixed pool of 10 threads, because the database has 10 connections.

2
00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:14.036
Each request queries the database, then calls an FX API for a rate.

3
00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:18.305
A burst of 300 arrives, and the pool takes 10 at a time.

4
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:22.188
So the database never sees more than 10 callers.

5
00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:25.309
The burst takes about 3,200 milliseconds.

6
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:30.087
The obvious migration is one line: a virtual thread per task.

7
00:00:30.087 --> 00:00:31.879
Now all 300 run at once.

8
00:00:31.879 --> 00:00:34.044
What happens at the database?

9
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:41.646
All 300 ask for a connection at once, and the pool waits only 30 milliseconds for one.

10
00:00:41.646 --> 00:00:44.200
That timeout was tuned for 10 callers.

11
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:46.488
Most of them time out.

12
00:00:46.488 --> 00:00:50.783
In one run, 71 requests worked and 229 failed.

13
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.552
The pool had two jobs.

14
00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:57.513
It ran the requests, and it kept the database to 10 callers.

15
00:00:57.513 --> 00:00:59.692
Nobody wrote the second job down.

16
00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:03.455
Virtual threads take over the first, and drop the second.

17
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:11.893
So put the second job back, on purpose: a semaphore with 10 permits, around the database call only.

18
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.605
Requests line up there, and the database gets 10 at a time.

19
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:19.303
The FX calls never needed a limit.

20
00:01:19.303 --> 00:01:23.588
They now overlap freely: about 200 in flight instead of 10.

21
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.457
The burst now takes about 260 milliseconds, with no failures.

22
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:34.835
Little's law tells you where requests wait: requests in flight equal throughput times time.

23
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:40.812
400 a second, for 0.12 seconds each, is about 48 in flight.

24
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:47.931
Turn it around: 10 permits, held 20 milliseconds each, serve at most 500 requests a second.

25
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.529
So what happens at 600 a second?

26
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:57.565
The queue on the semaphore grows on every reading, while throughput stays flat near 500.

27
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:02.189
A rising queue with flat throughput is what a full downstream looks like.

28
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:03.631
The rule.

29
00:02:03.631 --> 00:02:06.015
Removing the pool removes a limit.

30
00:02:06.015 --> 00:02:11.063
Write it down: one semaphore per downstream, sized from that downstream.

31
00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:12.342
Check yourself.

32
00:02:12.342 --> 00:02:16.856
To make it faster, someone raises the semaphore to 300 permits.

33
00:02:16.856 --> 00:02:19.579
The database still has 10 connections.

34
00:02:19.579 --> 00:02:21.299
What happens in a burst?

35
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.468
The limit is gone again: 300 callers reach 10 connections, and most time out.
